      "id": 2,
      "text": "Three potash mines near Esterhazy earned the town the nickname Potash Capital of the World.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tourismsaskatchewan.com/community/274/esterhazy",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1962, IMC Global completed a potash shaft that, together with its partner mine, now produces more potash than any other mine worldwide.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esterhazy%2C_Saskatchewan",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Esterhazy Flour Mill, built in 1905, was declared a national historic site of Canada in 2009.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esterhazy%2C_Saskatchewan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Hungarian farmers built the Kaposvar Church in 1906‑1907, and today it houses the Kaposvar Historic Site and Museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
